  "slides": [
    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "Real estate is a lease on a use case. Technology creates one enormous new use case — data centres — and erodes another — commodity offices. The sector's return depends less on construction technology than on power access, tenant credit and the ability to re-let space to machine and logistics workloads."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Returns come from rent, occupancy and cap-rate movement. Development earns a spread over cost; asset management earns on lease spreads and retention; leverage amplifies both.",
      "bullets": [
        "Net operating income is rent minus operating cost; occupancy and lease spreads drive it.",
        "Cap rates translate NOI into value and move with rates and risk appetite.",
        "Development margin is the spread between yield on cost and exit cap rate.",
        "Data centres earn on power density and contracted escalators.",
        "Office and retail face obsolescence risk that shows up in leasing spreads first."
      ]
    },
    {
